(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pulm-tuberculosis-drugs-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Tuberculosis Drugs Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is rifampin rarely used alone?","choices":["Its spectrum is narrow","It causes jaundice","It turns urine orange","Tolerance develops rapidly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin is rarely used alone because rapid tolerance develops; in tuberculosis it is always combined. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A woman who takes an oral contraceptive is starting rifampin for tuberculosis. What should she be told about the combination?","choices":["Her contraceptive may work less well","Her contraceptive levels will rise","Her contraceptive will weaken rifampin","The two drugs do not interact"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin induces cytochrome P450 enzymes and lowers contraceptive levels, so the contraceptive may fail; she needs counseling about pregnancy risk. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A patient with chronic liver disease needs tuberculosis therapy. Which first-line drug has no listed liver toxicity?","choices":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ethambutol's listed adverse effects are visual disturbances and raised uric acid; isoniazid, rifampin and pyrazinamide all list hepatitis. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"How does Mycobacterium tuberculosis become resistant to isoniazid?","choices":["Faster acetylation of the drug by the patient","Loss of the waxy mycolic acid layer of its wall","Mutation in the gene for its activating enzyme","Long periods of dormancy inside the host"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Resistance comes from mutation in the DNA coding for the activating enzyme, the bacterial catalase-peroxidase, so isoniazid is never activated. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which first-line tuberculosis drug should be avoided in pregnancy?","choices":["Isoniazid","Rifampin","Ethambutol","Pyrazinamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pyrazinamide is to be avoided in pregnancy; it also causes hepatitis and hyperuricemia. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What does isoniazid inhibit in Mycobacterium tuberculosis?","choices":["Mycolic acid production","DNA-dependent RNA polymerase","Mycolic acid incorporation","Growth at normal pH"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid inhibits mycolic acid production and so the waxy cell coat; it is bacteriostatic for latent and bactericidal for dividing bacteria. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Besides tuberculosis, rifampin is used as prophylaxis against which infection?","choices":["Influenza","Pneumococcal pneumonia","Endocarditis","Meningococcal disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin's primary use is tuberculosis, and it is also used prophylactically for meningococcal disease (and for endocarditis). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A patient starting isoniazid is also given pyridoxine (vitamin B6). Which adverse effect does the pyridoxine prevent?","choices":["Hepatitis","Peripheral neuritis","Lupus-like syndrome","Red-green color loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid's peripheral neuritis comes from pyridoxine deficiency, so pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is given with it. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which component of the mycobacterial cell wall forms its waxy coat?","choices":["Peptidoglycan","Mycolic acid","Arabinogalactan","Plasma membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mycobacteria are acid-fast bacilli whose distinct phosphoglycolipid layer, mycolic acid, forms the waxy cell coat. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Why is active tuberculosis never treated with a single drug?","choices":["To prevent resistance","To prevent liver toxicity","To reach the central nervous system","To shorten the dormant phase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Single agents are not used in active disease, to prevent resistance; rifampin and ethambutol in particular lose effect quickly when given alone. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which hypersensitivity reaction occurs in about 20 percent of patients taking rifampin?","choices":["Lupus-like syndrome","Peripheral neuritis","Flu-like syndrome","Visual disturbance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin hypersensitivity takes the form of a flu-like syndrome in about 20 percent of patients. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which visual change is a classic adverse effect of ethambutol?","choices":["Orange discoloration of tears","Decreased red-green discrimination","Dilated pupils with blurry vision","Yellowing of the whites of the eyes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ethambutol's visual disturbances are decreased red-green discrimination and decreased visual acuity. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Why is pyrazinamide given only during the first two months of tuberculosis treatment?","choices":["To avoid hyperuricemia","Because resistance is rapid","To avoid liver toxicity","Because it stains secretions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pyrazinamide is given for the first two months only, to avoid liver toxicity; it causes hepatitis in about 15 percent of patients. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"How long is the course of treatment for pulmonary tuberculosis?","choices":["2 months","6-9 months","9-12 months","18-24 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary tuberculosis is treated for 6 to 9 months, beginning with two months of four drugs and continuing with isoniazid and rifampin. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Isoniazid is activated by which bacterial enzyme?","choices":["RNA polymerase","Cytochrome P450 3A4","Phosphodiesterase","Catalase-peroxidase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Isoniazid is metabolically activated by the bacterial catalase-peroxidase; mutation in the DNA coding for this enzyme produces resistance. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How does pyrazinamide stop mycobacterial growth?","choices":["Its acid product lowers the pH","It inhibits RNA polymerase","It blocks mycolic acid synthesis","It weakens the cell wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pyrazinamide is converted to pyrazinoic acid, which lowers the pH so the bacteria cannot grow; it also enters macrophages. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 45"}]};
